erived from Article One that don't apply to the House is the Senate. Looking at the documents, Republicans took control of the Senate in 2015 (114th Congress) - this started in January 2015 according to lines 44 and 50.\n\nThe documents mention:\n- Line 44: \"With 248 seats in the House of Representatives and 54 seats in the Senate, this Congress began with the largest Republican majority since the 71st Congress of 1929 -- 1931.\" (referring to 104th Congress, 2015-2017)\n- Line 50: \"The Republicans regained the majority of the Senate in the 114th Congress, which started in January 2015; the Republicans had not controlled the Senate since January 2007.\"\n\nSo the answer is January 2015 (114th Congress) when Republicans regained control of the Senate.\n</think>\n\nBased on the documents, the body that has Standing Rules derived from Article One that do not apply to the House of Representatives is the **Senate**.\n\nAccording to the documents:\n- Line 11 describes how the Senate's Majority Leader manages the Senate in practice, in contrast to the House\n- Line 50 states: \"The Republicans regained the majority of the Senate in the **114th Congress, which started in January 2015**; the Republicans had not controlled the Senate since January 2007.\"\n\n**Answer: The Republican Party took control of the Senate on January 3, 2015**, when the 114th Congress began, with Republicans receiving 54 seats in the Senate after the 2014 elections.\n</task_result>","metadata":{"sessionId":"ses_21bea5cc3ffeZ0CG1SEcbifq2Y","model":{"modelID":"qwen35-9b","providerID":"local"},"truncated":false},"title":"Find when Republicans took Supreme Court control","time":{"start":1777648116536,"end":1777648143608}}}}
